  "final_text": "The dashboard and the alert rule both exist, and the rule is enabled. Its first run finished with status `ok`, so the query runs without errors. I couldn't confirm that the query actually matches 5xx documents: `KIBANA_TEST_API_KEY` is empty in my shell, so I had no way to search the index directly.\n\n**1. Fields in the data view** (`Kibana Sample Data Logs`, time field `timestamp`):\n- **Requests:** `request`, `url`, `extension`, `referer`\n- **Responses:** `response` holds the HTTP status code and is stored as text, with a `.keyword` subfield.\n- **Size:** `bytes`\n- **Clients:** `clientip`, `agent`, `machine.os`, `machine.ram`\n- **Location:** `geo.src`, `geo.dest`, `geo.coordinates`\n- **Other:** `host`, `tags`, `hour_of_day`\n\n**2. Dashboard \"EFF-8fa082be Web logs overview\"** (id `eff-8fa082be-web-logs-overview-80b1fb7ee0b9`) has 7 panels:\n- Total requests (count)\n- Unique visitors (unique count of `clientip`)\n- Total bytes served (sum of `bytes`)\n- Requests over time, split by response code\n- Response code breakdown (pie chart)\n- Top requested URLs, with request count and bytes\n- Traffic by source country (`geo.src`)\n\nURL: http://localhost:15601/app/dashboards#/view/eff-8fa082be-web-logs-overview-80b1fb7ee0b9\n\n**3. Rule \"EFF-8fa082be 5xx spike\"** (id `1c3f7ca2-a2a3-41d1-95f3-c39c818ec65e`) is an Elasticsearch query rule:\n- **Index and time field:** `kibana_sample_data_logs`, `timestamp`\n- **Query:** `response.keyword` in the range \"500\" to \"599\". Because the codes are stored as text, this is an alphabetical comparison; it still catches every 5xx code since they are all three digits.\n- **Condition:** fires when the count is more than 10 within the last 5 minutes\n- **Schedule:** runs every 1 minute\n\nThe rule has no actions attached, so when it fires it only records an alert in Kibana and doesn't notify anyone. Tell me where you want notifications to go if you'd like them.",
